Z||000008||stimpy||06-19-2000||08:47 PM||||Still, I can't help thinking this would be more for the benefit of people outside of the car then those inside. I can see if it was ported inside, but that would require cutting. Dowhatchalike, it's yer car, but I couldn't do it to mine.  I am looking forward to hearing about how it works for you though. Keep us posted. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Homicidal||06-19-2000||08:54 PM||||Well, I got one in the car already, and it sounds good, but it only provides "rear" bass due to it's extremly comfined area.<P>I need something to throw some frontal bass.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||06-19-2000||09:38 PM||||That's a good point. From the pics I've seen of your car, you have a wood box behind the seat. One, seat travel is limited, and the sound is probably getting mostly absorbed by the back of the seat or the firewall. I'm not an acoustical engineer (although I know we have one out here on the forum somewhere) but sound waves need to travel across air to do their business. I remember you posted how much better your stuff sounded when you had some space between the box and firewall.  Think how much it will really sound better if it has a whole cabin of airspace to get movin.<P>My suggestion, deepsix the box behind the seat. A fiberglass enclosure for the footwell is easy to make- even a jamoke like me can do it. The seat can go all the way back and there is still plenty of leg room. The sound is very clear and puchy. Even with only 75w RMS it has enough bass to quiver my nostril hairs!<P>Try it out. Test with your current box down in the footwell and see if it don't sound reeeel good. Sorry about the long post, but I do think it is the way to go. Peace my young bro.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Homicidal||06-19-2000||10:25 PM||||That would kill the passenger side since I already got the cd changer behind the seat.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||286SE'S||06-20-2000||07:02 PM||||The plastic shelf appears to be a drip tray for the AC dryer bottle. I have seen mine covered with condensation when the AC is running. The tray has a two inch wide slot in the middle which drains off to the outside.<BR>Cutting it out would give more room but your cabinet may get soggy on a hot day.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000013||BroCar 87||06-20-2000||09:32 PM||||Alright. Well I have a 10 inch in the passenger floorboard and a 10 inch in the front compartment. INSIDE the car, you can hear both. (the one in the floorboard better of course) OUTSIDE you can hear the one in the front really good, not so much the one on the floorboard. <BR>BUT HEY I am an 18 year old livin on the seat of my pants kinda guy. LOOKS AND STYLE are my main PRIORITY right now in life...lol<P>BUT ANYWAYS, The front compartment does rattle quite a bit. (tap on the plastic when you go out to your car. Where the underside of the sparetire would be... that rattles when it vibrates) <BR>I am going to DYNOMAT it and see if that helps, I am sure it will. <BR>The front is a good place IMO to put a sub for outside bass. (I like people to know when I am comin). WHEN THE HOOD IS DOWN, It seals the area where the speaker is. DEEPER SOUND. Sounds good if you ask me. <BR>As soon as My friend brings his digital camera over... I will take pics of both enclosures and quite a bit of other stuff for other threads.<BR>Thanks for the space...<BR>BroCar 87||||reg||1
